# ai-tools: EXAMPLE kernel audit rule -- every command a session runs.
# Not installed by ai-tools; copy it into /etc/audit/rules.d to enable
# it. `ai-tools audit` does not read it: the in-session entrypoint
# record that command reports is written by the SELinux policy, and
# this file is a separate, opt-in trail of every exec under the
# sandbox account. See "Read the logs" in the ai-tools docs.
#
# WHAT IT RECORDS. One event per execve by the sandbox account's uid,
# with the command (EXECVE argv), the working directory and the exec'd
# path. The filter is the uid alone, no SELinux field, so a policy
# reload does not change what it matches.
#
# SIZE IT BEFORE ENABLING IT. A busy session execs thousands of
# processes an hour at about 1-2 KB per event, so the default
# auditd.conf rotation (max_log_file = 8 MB, num_logs = 5) evicts the
# host's other audit records within hours. Raise both in
# /etc/audit/auditd.conf to hold the window you want to read back,
# for example a week of one busy session:
#
#     max_log_file = 64
#     num_logs = 20
#     max_log_file_action = ROTATE
#
# ENABLE. Substitute the sandbox account's name where it differs.
#
#     sudo cp /usr/share/ai-tools/audit/ai-tools-cmd.rules.example \
#         /etc/audit/rules.d/ai-tools-cmd.rules
#     sudo augenrules --load    # refused where the configuration is
#                               # immutable (-e 2): reboot instead
#
# READ BACK.
#
#     sudo ausearch -k ai-tools-cmd -ts today -i
#
# The file format takes one rule per line.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F uid=ai-tools -k ai-tools-cmd
